# The ISO inner identifier module (issue #706 §4) — the inner ISO document
# face as a referenceable library, NOT the declared ISO scheme (tier D
# stays coded; the study draft data/iso/historic-schemes.md defines the
# inner faces this module carries).
#
# A national-adoption definition binds this module so the adopted segment
# of `BS EN ISO 9001:2015` / `DIN ISO 12345` has a declared face and a
# declared identity: the adopted ISO document is the base of the wrapper.

module: iso.inner
version: 1
title: "The ISO inner identifier face"
description: >-
  The inner ISO document face an adoption names: the ISO publisher token,
  the document number with its part, and the year. The module carries the
  face, not the ISO scheme — the edition/supplement wave and the joint
  programs stay with the coded flavor (tier D, out of PFS scope).

grammar:
  iso_space: '" "'
  iso_colon: '":"'
  iso_dash: '"-"'
  iso_year: '4*digit'
  iso_part: '1*digit ["-" 1*digit]'
  iso_number: '1*("0" %x31-39) as iso_zero_lead *digit [iso_dash iso_part]'
  iso_document: '"ISO" iso_space iso_number [iso_colon iso_year]'

tokens:
  - "ISO"

identity:
  composition: wrapper-with-adopted
  base_module: iso.inner
  adopted_field: adopted

hooks:
  inner_parse: >-
    The adopted segment's parse. The inner ISO semantics (typed stages,
    supplements, the Components::Code number) are tier-D coded machinery;
    the v1 escape hatch is a coded hook beside the definition that the
    generated builder calls — explicit, never implicit.
